---
scope: src/service/install/provisionBrain
description: >
  Plans the brain-layer scaffolding as exactly TWO InitStep items —
  writes nothing to disk directly. Step 1 scaffolds
  `.aide/config/brain.aide` from the bundled template (default
  `obsidianBrainAideTemplate()`), with YAML null at every unwired slot
  in `mcpServerConfig.args`. Step 2 plans the `.mcp.json` brain entry
  derived from the scaffolded brain.aide via parseBrainAide +
  interpolateArgs. The four entry-point artifacts (playbook,
  study-playbook, update-playbook, research) and the brain root
  directory are NOT scaffolded by this module — they are seeded by the
  integration's `aide-config` body section prose at first
  `/aide:brain config` via the brain's own MCP write tools (e.g.
  `mcp__brain__write_note` for the bundled Obsidian default), reading
  the seed-section bytes via parseBrainAide's typed keys
  (`playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`).
  Takes no `brainPath` parameter at any layer; takes no slot-position
  knowledge of any kind. Inputs are the host project root, the MCP
  config path, and the on-disk bundled template bytes — nothing else.
  Invoked from two callers under the rework: `aide_init({ category:
  "brain" })` (fired by `/aide:brain config` for missing-`brain.aide`
  self-heal) and `cli/init` (fired at cold install to scaffold the
  bundled template into a fresh project; cli/init applies step 1 only,
  never step 2). The retired step builders — `buildBrainRootStep`,
  `buildPlaybookStep`, `buildStudyPlaybookStep`,
  `buildUpdatePlaybookStep`, `buildResearchStep` — are gone, and
  restoring any of them under any rename is the structural regression
  this spec forbids.
intent: >
  provisionBrain plans the brain-layer scaffolding the AIDE pipeline
  needs as exactly TWO InitStep records and writes nothing to disk
  directly — the install service's apply layer is the only writer.
  The two steps in fixed order are: (1) the brain config file at
  `.aide/config/brain.aide` (the user-owned configuration directory
  inherited from the root spec), scaffolded from the bundled template
  with YAML null at every unwired slot in `mcpServerConfig.args`;
  (2) the brain MCP server entry under the fixed `brain` key, derived
  from the scaffolded brain.aide via parseBrainAide + interpolateArgs
  (and carrying any null entries in args through unchanged). Inputs
  are the host project root, the MCP config path, and the on-disk
  bundled template bytes from `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`; this module
  takes NO `brainPath` parameter, NO slot-position knowledge, NO
  awareness of which arg index means what. The four entry-point
  artifacts (playbook, study-playbook, update-playbook, research) and
  the brain root directory are NOT scaffolded by this module — they
  are seeded by the integration's `aide-config` body section prose at
  first `/aide:brain config`, after the user fills the null slot(s)
  and runs sync, by reading the seed-section bytes from brain.aide
  via parseBrainAide's typed keys (`playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
  `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`) and writing each artifact via
  the brain's own MCP write tool (e.g. `mcp__brain__write_note` for
  the bundled Obsidian default) at paths the integration prose names.
  Slot-semantic, file-path, and write-tool-name knowledge ALL live in
  user-owned prose; provisionBrain is backend-blind. The module is
  invoked from two callers: `aide_init({ category: "brain" })` (fired
  by `/aide:brain config` for missing-`brain.aide` self-heal recovery)
  and `cli/init` (fired at cold install to scaffold the bundled
  brain.aide template into a fresh project). Both callers consume the
  two-step output via the apply layer's idempotent presence check on
  brain.aide; cli/init applies only step 1 (sync owns `.mcp.json`
  brain writes downstream), and `/aide:brain config` does not apply
  step 2 directly either — the integration prose wires the MCP entry
  by running sync after the user fills the null slot. The hand-off is
  load-bearing: provisionBrain plans, the agent fills, sync writes.
  The bundled template emits YAML null at unwired slots regardless of
  caller — `args` is typed `(string | null)[]` and YAML null is the
  explicit, structural unwired-slot signal carried by the schema
  itself, never a literal placeholder string. provisionBrain forwards
  the null verbatim through parseBrainAide and interpolateArgs into
  the derived MCP entry's args; sync, downstream, refuses to write
  null-bearing args. The brain.aide body carries six named
  marker-bounded sections per the parent body grammar, partitioned by
  who reads them: two live agent-facing sections (`orientation`,
  `config`) read by `aide_brain` at runtime, and four install-time-
  seed sections (`playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`,
  `researchIndex`) read by the integration prose at first
  `/aide:brain config` to seed entry-point artifacts in the brain.
  provisionBrain reads NEITHER category — the seed sections reach the
  brain via integration prose, not via this module's code. All code
  identifiers and prose in this module refer to the brain in
  storage-agnostic terms; the bundled `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`
  helper folder name is the only allowed exception, describing
  bundled template content rather than a code-level dispatch axis.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - >-
      "The brain config step plans creation of `.aide/config/brain.aide`
      inside the host project root as its own InitStep (category: brain,
      name: \"Brain config (brain.aide)\"). When absent, the step is
      would-create with a `content` field containing the canonical
      default brain.aide bytes from the bundled template helper
      (default `obsidianBrainAideTemplate()`), with the parent body
      grammar's six marker-bounded body sections in fixed order and
      YAML null at every unwired slot in `mcpServerConfig.args`. When
      present, the step is exists with no content field. The
      `.aide/config/` directory is the host's user-owned configuration
      boundary inherited from the root spec: any file scaffolded into
      it is owned by the user the moment it lands, and provisionBrain
      never returns would-overwrite for any path under `.aide/config/`.
      The invariant is structural — directory-level — not a per-file
      allowlist for brain.aide alone."
    - >-
      "The brain MCP server prescription is planned as the second (and
      final) InitStep under the fixed `brain` key. The MCP step's
      prescription.entry is derived from the scaffolded brain.aide
      bytes via parseBrainAide + interpolateArgs, not from a hardcoded
      launcher constant. The canonical default scaffolded brain.aide
      launches via `@bitbonsai/mcpvault` and supports both Windows
      (cmd /c npx ...) and POSIX (npx ...) per the template helper's
      platform branching. The bundled template emits YAML null at
      every unwired slot of `mcpServerConfig.args` regardless of
      caller input — `args` is typed `(string | null)[]` and YAML null
      is the explicit, structural unwired-slot signal — and
      provisionBrain forwards those nulls verbatim through parseBrainAide
      and interpolateArgs into the derived MCP entry's args. The
      InitStep captures the file shape with null entries intact; the
      actual `.mcp.json` write happens via sync downstream, and sync's
      contract is to refuse to write null-bearing args. The hand-off
      is load-bearing: provisionBrain plans, the agent fills the
      null(s) by direct edit to brain.aide (driven verbatim by the
      integration's `config` body section prose, executed by
      `/aide:brain config`), then sync writes the result. The default
      scaffold makes no use of `${...}` substitution; the parser
      still supports `${...}` interpolation of frontmatter fields
      inside `mcpServerConfig.args` for advanced users who want to DRY
      a value across positions, but that surface is not exercised by
      the default scaffold. The step reports would-create on cold
      install (no `brain` key in `.mcp.json`), would-overwrite when
      the existing `brain` entry diverges from the derived command
      and args, and exists when the `brain` key is already present
      with an entry that matches the derived command and args."
    - >-
      "provisionBrain returns exactly TWO InitStep items in fixed
      order — the brain.aide scaffold step first, the MCP entry plan
      step second — and nothing else. There is no third step builder,
      no fourth, no seventh; the two-step shape is the contract.
      The four entry-point artifacts (playbook, study-playbook,
      update-playbook, research) and the brain root directory are NOT
      scaffolded by any step in this module's pipeline. They are
      seeded by the integration's `aide-config` body section prose at
      first `/aide:brain config`, after the user fills the null
      slot(s) and runs sync, by reading the seed-section bytes from
      brain.aide via parseBrainAide's typed keys (`playbookIndex`,
      `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`) and writing
      each artifact via the brain's own MCP write tool (e.g.
      `mcp__brain__write_note` for the bundled Obsidian default) at
      paths the integration prose names. provisionBrain's code path
      stops at scaffolding the bundled brain.aide template plus
      planning the `.mcp.json` brain entry; everything downstream of
      that lives in user-owned prose."
    - >-
      "provisionBrain takes no `brainPath` parameter at any layer —
      not on the orchestrator, not on any per-step builder, not on
      the bundled template helper. Its inputs are the host project
      root, the MCP config path, and the on-disk bundled template
      bytes from `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`. The bundled template
      ships with YAML null at every unwired slot of
      `mcpServerConfig.args` regardless of any caller input; YAML
      null is the structural unwired-slot signal carried by the typed
      `(string | null)[]` schema, never a literal sentinel string.
      The brain root path reaches sync (and through sync, `.mcp.json`)
      only by the user (or the agent acting on their behalf via the
      integration's `config` body-section prose) editing it into
      brain.aide post-scaffold; provisionBrain re-reads it on the
      next call via parseBrainAide. Adding a `brainPath` parameter to
      any of this module's surfaces — orchestrator signature,
      per-step builder, helper — restores the retired in-code
      path-routing the schema collapse already retired."
    - >-
      "provisionBrain does not introspect `mcpServerConfig.args` to
      extract slot semantics. It reads `args` only as opaque bytes —
      passing them byte-for-byte through parseBrainAide and
      interpolateArgs into the MCP step's prescription, including any
      YAML null entries unchanged. It never reads `args[N]` for any
      named integer N to extract a brain root path, validate a path,
      check a token shape, sniff connector identity, or branch on
      slot meaning. Slot-semantic knowledge — which arg index is a
      path, which is a token, which is a URI — lives exclusively in
      the per-integration `config` body section, owned by the user.
      provisionBrain is backend-blind at the slot level."
    - >-
      "provisionBrain is invoked from exactly two callers under the
      rework, and consumes the two-step output the same way at each:
      (1) `aide_init({ category: \"brain\" })`, fired by
      `/aide:brain config` UNCONDITIONALLY at the top of every
      invocation (so missing-`brain.aide` self-heals via the
      idempotent presence check on step 1); (2) `cli/init`, fired at
      cold install to scaffold the bundled brain.aide template into
      a fresh project (a new caller under this rework — cli/init was
      previously brain-agnostic). Both callers apply step 1 (write
      brain.aide) only when the file is absent — presence is the
      idempotency signal, byte drift is not. cli/init does NOT apply
      step 2 (sync owns `.mcp.json` brain writes downstream);
      `/aide:brain config` does not apply step 2 directly either —
      the integration prose wires the MCP entry by running sync after
      the user fills the null slot. The shape of the InitStep output
      is identical regardless of caller; consumer-shape detail of how
      each caller applies which subset of the two steps is the
      caller's concern, not provisionBrain's."
    - >-
      "Brain.aide is scaffolded with idempotent presence-only
      checking. When the file already exists at
      `.aide/config/brain.aide`, the step is exists regardless of
      byte drift from the bundled template — once the file lands,
      the user owns it forever and provisionBrain never proposes
      replacing it. The presence-only rule applies to brain.aide
      uniformly across both callers (`aide_init({ category: \"brain\"
      })` and `cli/init`); a missing `brain.aide` self-heals on the
      next call by re-scaffolding the bundled template in place,
      a customized `brain.aide` is left alone forever."
    - >-
      "All code identifiers and inline prose in this module refer to
      the brain in storage-agnostic terms. The two structural concepts
      the module handles are the brain config file scaffold and the
      brain MCP entry plan; how the brain itself manifests in the
      user's chosen storage (Markdown files in an editor-backed brain,
      pages in a hosted knowledge base, plain folders) is brain.aide's
      concern, not this module's. Methodology guidance about how
      agents navigate or maintain the brain (link-crawling protocols,
      search-vs-skill routing, entry-point traversal rules,
      playbook-maintenance recipes) lives only in pipeline prompts,
      brain.aide body sections (notably the user-owned `studyPlaybook`
      and `updatePlaybook` section bytes that the bundled obsidian
      default ships), and methodology-doc prose surfaces — never in
      code identifiers or inline string constants in this module."
    - >-
      "This spec's prose, outcome text, and references use
      storage-agnostic vocabulary per the root spec's forbidden-token
      outcome. The bundled template helper folder name
      `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` is the only allowed exception; it
      describes bundled template content rather than a code-level
      branch target."
    - Every InitStep returned is a planning artifact only — no files are
      written to disk by this helper. The install service's apply layer
      handles all writes; whichever consumer (the `aide_init` MCP tool
      via `/aide:brain config`, or `cli/init` at cold install) drives
      the apply layer is consumer-shape detail, not part of this
      helper's contract.
  undesired:
    - >-
      "Returning more than two InitStep items from this module's
      pipeline. The two-step shape — brain.aide scaffold + MCP entry
      plan — is the contract; a three-step, four-step, five-step,
      six-step, or seven-step shape under any rationale (\"we need a
      step for the brain root,\" \"we need a step per entry-point
      artifact,\" \"we need a step for the playbook index\") restores
      the retired pipeline shape and is rejected. The four entry-point
      artifacts and the brain root directory are NOT this module's
      responsibility; they are seeded by the integration's
      `aide-config` body section prose at first `/aide:brain config`,
      via the brain's own MCP write tools."
    - >-
      "Restoring any of the five retired step builders —
      `buildBrainRootStep`, `buildPlaybookStep`,
      `buildStudyPlaybookStep`, `buildUpdatePlaybookStep`,
      `buildResearchStep` — under any name. The regression class is
      structural, not name-bound: a step builder that creates the
      brain root directory (under any rename: `seedBrainRoot`,
      `ensureBrainRoot`, `mkBrainDir`, etc.) is the same regression
      as `buildBrainRootStep`; a step builder that reads a seed body
      section (`playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, or
      `researchIndex`) and writes its bytes to a path inside the
      brain (under any rename: `seedPlaybook`, `writePlaybookArtifact`,
      `scaffoldStudyPlaybook`, `landResearchEntry`,
      `provisionUpdatePlaybook`, etc.) is the same regression as the
      four retired entry-point step builders. Either shape is
      rejected regardless of identifier."
    - >-
      "Any code path that introspects which arg slot in
      `mcpServerConfig.args` means what. A `PATH_SLOT_INDEX` constant
      or any other named integer hardcoding a slot position; a
      `resolveBrainRoot`-style helper that reads
      `mcpServerConfig.args[N]` for any N to extract a brain root path
      or any other slot semantic; a switch on `args[3]` because
      Obsidian's path lives there; a matcher recognizing token-shaped
      strings at `args[2]`; an `argSlots` frontmatter field declaring
      \"this index is a path\"; any code path that reads
      `mcpServerConfig.args[N]` for any reason other than byte-faithful
      forwarding through interpolateArgs — all forbidden. Slot
      semantics live exclusively in the per-integration `config` body
      section, owned by the user; the package never inspects which
      slot is which. A helper or constant that grows arg-index
      awareness is the in-code backend registry coming back through
      the slot surface."
    - Constructing the MCP entry inline without going through the scaffolded
      brain.aide as the source of truth — the MCP step's prescription must
      always derive from parsed brain.aide bytes via parseBrainAide +
      interpolateArgs.
    - >-
      "Scaffolding entry-point artifact bytes from inline TypeScript
      string constants in this module — playbook-index-section bytes,
      study-playbook-section bytes, update-playbook-section bytes,
      research-index-section bytes, or any future install-time-seed
      section's bytes belong in the named body sections of the bundled
      default brain.aide and nowhere in this module's code. Inline
      `*_TEMPLATE` consts (one per entry-point artifact) are the
      retired pre-pivot shape; shipping an inline `UPDATE_PLAYBOOK_TEMPLATE`
      (or `STUDY_PLAYBOOK_TEMPLATE`, or any other install-time-seed
      constant) alongside any sibling is the same regression in different
      clothing as the launcher-bytes duplication the schema collapse
      already retired. Adding a new install-time-seed section to
      brain.aide in a future amendment never permits a \"temporary\"
      inline copy pending a brain.aide migration."
    - >-
      "Scaffolding a brain-root CLAUDE.md as part of the brain
      contract. The framework's brain contract is exactly four
      required entry-point artifacts (playbook + study-playbook +
      update-playbook + research) seeded by integration prose, NOT by
      this module; a brain-root CLAUDE.md is no longer a framework
      requirement and provisionBrain emits no InitStep for it under
      any name — \"vault CLAUDE.md\", \"brain CLAUDE.md\", \"navigation
      hub\", or otherwise. (The tokens \"vault\" and \"hub\" appear here
      only to forbid the retired naming, per the root spec's
      anti-example carve-out.)"
    - >-
      "Using storage-specific terminology in code identifiers, type names,
      function names, file names, JSDoc, or inline string constants in
      this module. The forbidden tokens — `obsidian`, `vault`, `hub`,
      `hub note`, `wikilink`, and `note` (used as a synonym for \"file\")
      — all encode a connector-specific mental model the framework no
      longer assumes. Code refers to the brain root, the brain config
      file, and the MCP entry in storage-agnostic terms; the bundled
      default brain.aide remains Obsidian-flavored, but that is a
      template-content fact, not a code-identifier fact. The Obsidian
      default is the most common starting point, never a code-level
      special case the module branches on. Allowed exception: the
      `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` helper folder name (describes
      bundled content, not a dispatch axis)."
    - >-
      "Embedding methodology prose — link-crawling protocols,
      entry-point-vs-content depth rules, decision protocols (\"use
      the study-playbook skill for X, search_notes for Y\"),
      playbook-maintenance recipes, routing-table drift-detection
      mechanics, where-to-find-things tables, or any prose that uses
      the forbidden tokens (`hub`, `wikilink`, `vault`,
      `note`-as-synonym, `hub note`) — as inline string constants in
      this module's code. The multi-month-tested navigation
      methodology lives in the bundled brain.aide template helper's
      `studyPlaybook` body section bytes only; the playbook-maintenance
      methodology lives in the bundled brain.aide template helper's
      `updatePlaybook` body section bytes only. provisionBrain does
      not read those bytes (the integration prose at first
      `/aide:brain config` does); duplicating them as a sibling
      inline TypeScript constant in this module's code is the same
      regression in different clothing — the bytes live in one place,
      the user-owned section. That prose otherwise belongs in
      pipeline prompts and the methodology docs the strategist owns;
      baking it into install code couples the install pipeline to a
      specific brain navigation or maintenance paradigm and forces
      every change in methodology to ship as a code release."
    - "Hardcoding the `@bitbonsai/mcpvault` launcher shape outside of the
      bundled default brain.aide template helper — the launcher bytes
      belong in the template helper and nowhere else in this module.
      Reintroducing an inline `obsidian-mcp` shape (the legacy launcher)
      is the same regression in different clothing: any duplication of
      launcher bytes outside the template is rejected."
    - "Scaffolding a brain.aide whose `name` value the package later
      branches on — the name field is a human-readable label for the user
      and the agent only; no code in this package may dispatch on it. Any
      code path that reads `name` to pick a backend, render
      connector-specific prose, or alter the MCP step is the in-code
      backend registry coming back through the side door."
    - >-
      "Scaffolding a brain.aide whose frontmatter contains any field beyond
      `name` and `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}` — no `connector`, no
      `rootPath`, no `entryFile`, no `tools` block, no `argSlots`. Those
      fields were removed by the schema collapse; reintroducing any of
      them adds a validation-theater field with no real runtime consumer
      (sync writes `mcpServerConfig`; the user and agent read `name` as
      a label) and forces the parser to either ignore it or fail on it.
      The schema minimum is the schema maximum."
    - >-
      "Scaffolding a default brain.aide whose `mcpServerConfig.args`
      uses `${rootPath}` (or any other `${...}` placeholder) at any
      unwired slot, OR uses ANY literal-string sentinel —
      `<BRAIN_PATH>`, `<PATH>`, `__BRAIN__`, an empty string, or any
      other shape — at any unwired slot. The unwired-slot signal is
      YAML null, full stop: `mcpServerConfig.args` is typed
      `(string | null)[]` and the bundled template emits null at every
      unwired slot. `${...}` interpolation remains the opt-in
      advanced-user surface for DRYing values across positions, but is
      not exercised by the default scaffold. Shipping any literal-string
      placeholder forces a sentinel-detection code path somewhere
      downstream, converts a structural unwired-slot signal into a
      string the tooling has to recognize, and re-couples the install
      pipeline to a 'knows what an unwired path looks like' code path;
      the structural-null approach avoids that exact tax."
    - >-
      "Replacing presence-only idempotency on brain.aide with byte
      comparison against the bundled template — brain.aide is
      seed-semantic the moment it lands; presence is the only
      idempotency signal, and any subsequent run that proposes
      would-overwrite for `.aide/config/brain.aide` shifts a
      load-bearing user-owned surface out from under the user. Same
      class of failure as the MCP server silently rewriting
      `.mcp.json`."
    - >-
      "Returning a would-overwrite step for any path under `.aide/config/`,
      regardless of which file it is — same class of failure as the MCP
      server silently rewriting `.mcp.json`. The `.aide/config/` directory
      is a load-bearing user-owned surface inherited from the root spec.
      Per-file allowlists are forbidden; the directory boundary is the
      ownership signal, and any code path that touches a file under
      `.aide/config/` after its first write — even to \"fix\" a stale
      template, land newer defaults, or tidy formatting — is rejected."
    - "Writing a brain.aide whose schema borrows intent-spec frontmatter
      conventions — no `scope`, no `outcomes`, no `intent`, no `status`
      lifecycle field. brain.aide is a config file, not an intent spec;
      conflating the two erodes the distinction every downstream agent
      depends on."
    - >-
      "Carrying a `brainPath` parameter on the orchestrator
      signature, on any per-step builder, or on the bundled template
      helper. The bundled template emits YAML null at unwired slots
      regardless of caller input; provisionBrain has no `brainPath`
      input to pass through and no branch to make on its presence or
      absence. Any `(projectRoot, brainPath?, mcpConfigPath)`-shaped
      signature, any `if (brainPath) { /* one entry */ } else { /* a
      different entry */ }` shape, and any helper-helper
      pass-through of a path argument restore the pre-pivot
      caller-driven path-routing the schema retired. This invariant
      holds across BOTH callers (`aide_init({ category: \"brain\" })`
      and `cli/init`): neither passes a brain path; cli/init is
      argument-less for brain wiring, and brain MCP entry wiring is
      delegated entirely to `/aide:brain config` on the first
      `/aide` run."
    - >-
      "Substituting any other shape for the YAML null at any unwired
      slot of `mcpServerConfig.args` — replacing null with an empty
      string, dropping the null entry from the array entirely, or
      converting it into a literal-string sentinel
      (`<BRAIN_PATH>`, `<PATH>`, `__BRAIN__`, etc.) anywhere along
      the chain (template helper, parseBrainAide, interpolateArgs,
      MCP-step InitStep). The structural unwired-slot signal is
      typed `(string | null)[]` and a YAML null is the only valid
      unwired marker; converting it into a string at any layer
      defeats sync's null-detection contract and silently lets a
      partially-wired entry land in `.mcp.json`."
    - An InitStep that writes to disk directly. provisionBrain is a
      planner; any step that touches the filesystem violates the
      architectural contract between planning helpers and the apply layer.
---

## Context

provisionBrain plans the brain-layer scaffolding the AIDE pipeline
needs on first install and on every subsequent self-heal run. The
framework's brain contract is exactly four required entry-point
artifacts — a playbook entry-point artifact, a study-playbook
entry-point artifact, an update-playbook entry-point artifact, and a
research entry-point artifact — plus the MCP wiring that connects
the host's agents to whatever knowledge store backs them.
provisionBrain's job is to plan seven InitStep records that together
stand up that contract in a fixed order: the user-editable
`brain.aide` config file at `.aide/config/brain.aide`, the brain
root directory tree, the playbook entry-point artifact, the
study-playbook entry-point artifact, the update-playbook entry-point
artifact, the research entry-point artifact, and the brain MCP
server entry under the fixed `brain` key. It writes nothing to disk
— every step is a planning artifact the install service's apply
layer turns into bytes.

provisionBrain is invoked exclusively by `aide_init({ category:
"brain" })`. `cli/init` is argument-less and brain-agnostic and does
not call this module; the brain category is reached only through
the `aide_init` MCP tool, and only one call site does so:
`/aide:brain config`. That router calls `aide_init({ category:
"brain" })` UNCONDITIONALLY at the top of every invocation — the
call is not gated on `aide_info.brain.status`, not branched on
whether `brain.aide` already exists, not skipped when entry-point
artifacts are present. The unconditional call is what enables
missing-file recovery: if a seed entry-point artifact was deleted
from the brain root by accident, if a prior install was interrupted
mid-write, or if a template-version bump added a new required
artifact, the next `/aide:brain config` invocation re-seeds the
missing files in place. Presence-only seed-semantic idempotency
makes that self-heal safe: every step that is already present
returns `exists` without touching customized bytes; only the
genuinely-absent step lands new content.

The load-bearing structural fact about `brain.aide`'s body is the
two-category split by reader. The body carries six named
marker-bounded sections in fixed order. Two of them — `orientation`
and `config` — are live agent-facing sections that `aide_brain`
reads at runtime to deliver the runtime briefing and the
integration-specific wiring flow. The other four — `playbookIndex`,
`studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, and `researchIndex` — are
install-time seeds that this module reads exactly once per install
run and writes into the brain root as files. After install, the
live source for each seed artifact is the on-disk file in the brain,
not the brain.aide section that originated it; the brain.aide bytes
go dormant for those four sections. The two categories are strict
siblings with no cross-reading: `aide_brain` does not read the seed
keys, and provisionBrain does not read `orientation` or `config`.
Cross-reading would re-create the in-code backend registry the
architecture rejects — the per-consumer ownership boundary is what
keeps each section's authority unambiguous and lets each
integration's prose evolve without coordinating with the other
consumer.

The typed-result keys read by this module are `playbookIndex`,
`studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, and `researchIndex`. The "index"
suffix on `playbookIndex` and `researchIndex` calls out that those
sections seed navigable index surfaces (a Task Routing table, a
domain index — surfaces the user fills with their own content); the
unsuffixed `studyPlaybook` and `updatePlaybook` keys carry
package-authored methodology prose (how to read the playbook, how
to maintain it). The retired key vocabulary (`prose`, `playbook` as
a typed key, `research` as a typed key) is gone from this module's
read paths; the rename is load-bearing because it disambiguates each
section's role and the index-vs-methodology distinction the four
seed sections turn on.

The brain root path is not a parameter. provisionBrain takes no
`brainPath` input on its orchestrator signature, on any per-step
builder, or on the bundled template helper. Its inputs are the host
project root, the MCP config path, and the on-disk bundled template
bytes from `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`. The bundled template ships
with YAML null at the path slot of `mcpServerConfig.args` — `args`
is typed `(string | null)[]`, and YAML null is the explicit,
structural unwired-slot signal carried by the schema itself. Per-
integration body sections name which arg index means what (for the
bundled Obsidian default, `args[3]` is the absolute path to the
user's brain root); the package never introspects slot semantics.
The brain-root-dependent steps derive the brain root by reading the
on-disk brain.aide via parseBrainAide; when the path slot reports
null, the brain root is unknown and the dependent steps reflect
that. The MCP step's prescription is derived from the same parsed
brain.aide via parseBrainAide + interpolateArgs and may carry null
entries through unchanged into the derived entry's args.

The hand-off is load-bearing: provisionBrain plans, the agent fills,
sync writes. provisionBrain plans the seven InitStep records as a
consistent file-shape contract regardless of whether the path slot
is null or filled. The downstream apply layer writes the file-system
steps (brain config, directories, entry-point artifacts) the same
way regardless. The MCP entry, however, reaches `.mcp.json` via
sync, and sync's contract is to refuse to write null-bearing args
— so the MCP step staged with nulls is staged-only until the agent
fills the null(s) in by direct edit to brain.aide, driven verbatim
by the integration's `config` body section prose that
`/aide:brain config` executes. Once the agent has filled the null,
sync writes the result. provisionBrain itself never inspects the
slot, never substitutes a fallback, and never branches on the
null-vs-string state.

The brain.aide path lives under `.aide/config/`, the host's
user-owned configuration directory established by the root spec.
The ownership rule is directory-wide: any file scaffolded into
`.aide/config/` is owned by the user the moment it lands, and no
subsequent run overwrites, patches, or migrates anything inside
that directory. brain.aide is the current inhabitant; the
convention is forward-compatible with future user-owned config
files, and provisionBrain inherits the structural rule rather than
restating a per-file invariant.

The framework's brain contract does NOT include a brain-root
CLAUDE.md. A previous shape returned a CLAUDE.md scaffolding step;
that step has been retired and the framework's brain contract is
exactly four required entry-point artifacts (playbook +
study-playbook + update-playbook + research) — nothing else. Users
who want a CLAUDE.md inside their brain are free to add one — it is
their brain — but the framework does not require it, and
provisionBrain emits no InitStep for it. The methodology guidance
that used to live in that file (link-crawling protocols, decision
protocols, where-to-find-things tables) now flows through the
`studyPlaybook` body section and the on-disk artifact it scaffolds;
the shipped `/aide:update-playbook` command similarly points at the
`updatePlaybook`-seeded artifact for playbook-maintenance
methodology. The strategist owns the prose, the bundled template
ships it, and the install module reads it back through
parseBrainAide rather than baking it into install code.

## Strategy

**Seven planning steps in fixed order: brain config → brain root
directories → playbook entry-point artifact → study-playbook
entry-point artifact → update-playbook entry-point artifact →
research entry-point artifact → MCP entry.** Each is its own
InitStep with its own idempotency check, and the order is
load-bearing. Brain config is planned first because every step
downstream of it derives content from the bytes it scaffolds. The
four entry-point artifact steps come before the MCP step because
the same parser pass that powers all five reads the same
brain.aide; running parseBrainAide once after the brain config step
is resolved keeps the orchestrator's flow linear. The artifact-step
order — playbook → study-playbook → update-playbook → research —
mirrors the install-time-seed body-section order in brain.aide
(`playbookIndex` → `studyPlaybook` → `updatePlaybook` →
`researchIndex`), with the two `coding-playbook/*` siblings grouped
ahead of the lone `research/*` sibling so the artifact-step list
walks the brain root one directory at a time. The update-playbook
step is a sibling of the study-playbook step because both seed
methodology files into the same `coding-playbook/` directory and
share the same shape (a methodology file, not an index).

**provisionBrain takes no `brainPath` parameter at any layer,
because cli/init no longer calls it.** The pre-pivot shape carried
an optional `brainPath` from the cold-install CLI down through this
module so that a real path could be inlined into
`mcpServerConfig.args` at scaffold time. The current shape moves
brain wiring entirely into `/aide:brain config` on first `/aide`
run: cli/init is argument-less and brain-agnostic, and provisionBrain
is reached only by `aide_init({ category: "brain" })` from
`/aide:brain config`. With no caller-supplied path to thread,
`brainPath` is not just optional — it does not exist at any layer.
The orchestrator signature, the per-step builders, and the bundled
template helper all take only the inputs they actually need: the
host project root, the MCP config path, and the bundled template
bytes. Carrying a `brainPath` argument anywhere in this module
would restore the in-code path-routing the schema collapse retired
and reintroduce a caller surface no consumer would call.

**The unwired-slot signal is YAML null, not a literal-string
sentinel.** The bundled obsidian template emits YAML null at the
path slot of `mcpServerConfig.args` regardless of caller input —
`args` is typed `(string | null)[]`, the typing is the contract,
and YAML null is the explicit, structural unwired-slot signal. The
prior shape used a literal-string placeholder at the same slot,
which had a load-bearing failure mode: the placeholder string was
byte-stable across reads, so an MCP step computed against it would
match an existing `.mcp.json` entry that also carried the
placeholder verbatim — the drift check passed, sync wrote the
placeholder, and the brain MCP server failed to launch until the
user noticed and re-ran `/aide:brain config`. Structural null
closes that trap at the schema layer: sync's contract is to refuse
to write null-bearing args, so a partially-wired brain configuration
cannot silently land in `.mcp.json`. The package never has to
recognize a string the agent or template chose as the "unwired"
marker; the typed schema carries that signal directly.

**Per-consumer ownership of brain.aide body sections is enforced
strictly at the typed-key level.** The brain.aide body partitions
into two reader categories. `aide_brain` reads `orientation` and
`config` at runtime; provisionBrain reads only the four
install-time-seed keys (`playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
`updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`) via parseBrainAide. The boundary
is enforced at the typed-key level: any code path in this module
that names `orientation` or `config` as a parseBrainAide result
field, dereferences either, or passes either to a downstream
helper crosses the per-consumer-ownership line and is rejected —
even transitively, even to validate the parser's result, even to
log. The symmetric inverse holds in `aide_brain`'s spec (its
consumer never reads the seed keys), so the four seed sections
reach the agent only through the on-disk artifacts this module
plans. Cross-reading would re-create the in-code backend registry
the architecture rejects: each integration's `orientation` and
`config` prose can evolve independently of its seed-section prose,
and conflating the two readers reintroduces the coupling the
schema collapse retired.

**brain.aide is the single source of truth for both launcher bytes
and seed-artifact bytes.** Every artifact step sources its
`content` from the corresponding install-time-seed body section
via parseBrainAide. The MCP step calls parseBrainAide (or its
in-memory string sibling for the cold-install path where
brain.aide has not yet hit disk) and then `interpolateArgs` to
compute the entry's command and args. The four entry-point
artifact steps call the same parser and read `playbookIndex`,
`studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, and `researchIndex` for their
bytes. No artifact step hardcodes a launcher shape, a playbook
structure, a study-playbook navigation methodology, an
update-playbook maintenance methodology, or a research structure
as an inline TypeScript constant — every byte the apply layer
eventually writes for these artifacts traces back to a body
section the user owns and can edit. A user edit to any seed
section flows into the next install or sync automatically; the
package no longer competes with the user for ownership of any of
these bytes.

**Body sections are marker-bounded with a closed marker set; the
install-time-seed typed keys are `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
`updatePlaybook`, and `researchIndex`.** The parser enforces six
required marker pairs in fixed order:
`<!-- aide-orientation-start -->` / `<!-- aide-orientation-end -->`,
`<!-- aide-config-start -->` / `<!-- aide-config-end -->`,
`<!-- aide-playbook-index-start -->` /
`<!-- aide-playbook-index-end -->`,
`<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->` /
`<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->`,
`<!-- aide-update-playbook-start -->` /
`<!-- aide-update-playbook-end -->`, and
`<!-- aide-research-index-start -->` /
`<!-- aide-research-index-end -->` — recognized lowercase and
case-sensitive in the orientation-then-config-then-playbookIndex-
then-studyPlaybook-then-updatePlaybook-then-researchIndex order.
The parsed result on the `ok` branch carries each section as its
own typed field whose name maps from the kebab-case marker base
name to a camelCase typed key. Marker layout violations (a missing
pair, a typo'd token, an unmatched closer, an opener without a
closer, wrong section order, nested markers) return
`malformed-body` naming the violating marker — there is no
recovery, no auto-fill, no silent default. The closed marker set
means provisionBrain reaches for the section it owns by typed-key
name and never re-implements body grammar.

**Plans / agent fills / sync writes is the load-bearing hand-off
shape.** provisionBrain plans the seven InitStep records for any
brain.aide it parses — including a brain.aide whose path slot is
YAML null. The downstream apply layer writes the file-system steps
(brain config, directories, entry-point artifacts) the same way
regardless of slot state. The MCP step, however, reaches `.mcp.json`
via sync, and sync refuses to write null-bearing args by contract.
That is the load-bearing safety net: provisionBrain plans, the
agent fills the null in via direct edit to brain.aide (driven
verbatim by the integration's `config` body section prose, which
`/aide:brain config` executes via `aide_brain({ kind: "config" })`),
and sync writes the result. provisionBrain itself never inspects
the slot, never substitutes a fallback, and never branches on
null-vs-string. Defensive null-replacement here would re-couple the
install pipeline to a "knows what an unwired path looks like" code
path and would mask user mistakes that the sync-refusal surfaces
loudly. The structural-null signal flows verbatim from the bundled
template through parseBrainAide, through interpolateArgs, into the
MCP-step InitStep, until sync (downstream, after the agent has
filled it) writes a real string to `.mcp.json`.

**Presence-only seed-semantic idempotency on every seed step lets
the unconditional `aide_init` call self-heal missing files.**
brain.aide and the four entry-point artifacts use the same
idempotency rule: if the file exists at its expected path, the step
is `exists` — no byte comparison, no drift detection against the
brain.aide section that originated it. The rule is what makes
`/aide:brain config`'s unconditional `aide_init({ category:
"brain" })` call safe to run on every invocation: a customized
brain.aide returns `exists` and is left alone; a customized
entry-point artifact (the user has been editing their playbook for
weeks) returns `exists` and is left alone; only a genuinely-absent
seed file (deleted by accident, missing because a prior run was
interrupted, missing because a template-version bump added a new
required artifact) returns `would-create` and the apply layer
re-seeds it. Byte-comparison idempotency would do the opposite —
every customization would surface as `would-overwrite`, every
re-run would prompt "overwrite your seed?", and the unconditional
self-heal would degrade into a continual nag. The brain MCP
prescription uses canonical-derived idempotency instead because
its bytes belong to the package, not the user: the expected entry
is computed from the current brain.aide via parseBrainAide +
interpolateArgs and compared against `.mcp.json`. Divergence means
`would-overwrite`; the rule applies only to the MCP step.

**`.aide/config/` is a directory-wide user-owned boundary.** Any
file scaffolded into `.aide/config/` is owned by the user the
moment it lands. provisionBrain never returns `would-overwrite`
for any path under `.aide/config/`. The rule is structural — at
the directory level, not a per-file allowlist for brain.aide
alone. brain.aide is the current inhabitant; future config files
inherit the same posture without the rule needing to be restated
per file.

**Code identifiers and inline prose stay storage-agnostic.** No
identifier in `index.ts` — function name, variable name, parameter
name, file path constant, JSDoc word, log string — uses any of the
forbidden tokens (`vault`, `obsidian`, `note`-as-synonym,
`wikilink`, `hub note`, `hub`). The four structural concepts the
module handles are the playbook entry-point artifact, the
study-playbook entry-point artifact, the update-playbook
entry-point artifact, and the research entry-point artifact. The
update-playbook step's builder is `buildUpdatePlaybookStep`
(storage-agnostic, mirrors `buildPlaybookStep`,
`buildStudyPlaybookStep`, and `buildResearchStep`); the artifact
path constant lives inside the bundled `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`
and resolves to `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md` for an
editor-backed Markdown brain — that is a template-content fact,
not a code-level dispatch axis. The parent intent's user-owned-
section-bytes carve-out applies to every install-time-seed
section's bytes (the bundled obsidian-flavored default legitimately
ships connector-specific prose inside `studyPlaybook` and
`updatePlaybook` per that carve-out, and may ship connector-
specific phrasing inside `playbookIndex` and `researchIndex`'s
scaffolded structure too). The carve-out applies to the BYTES
(which become an on-disk artifact the user can edit and override
for any other brain), not to provisionBrain's code surface.

**Migration semantics for pre-rework brain.aide files are strict
failure, not auto-rewrite.** Cold installs land in the new shape
automatically — the bundled template carries all six marker-bounded
body sections from day one with YAML null at the path slot. Any
pre-rework shape (heading-based body, four-section marker-bounded
body with the retired marker names, brain.aide carrying a literal-
string placeholder rather than YAML null at the path slot, or
brain.aide whose frontmatter still names retired fields like
`connector`/`rootPath`) surfaces as `malformed-body` from
parseBrainAide because the parser's body grammar is closed.
provisionBrain does not silently patch the file under
`.aide/config/` to rename markers, fill missing sections, or
convert string placeholders to null — that would violate the
directory-wide ownership rule and re-introduce a "package knows
how the user's config should look" code path. The user hand-edits
their brain.aide to align with the new shape; the strict-failure
path is intentional. Until the user completes the migration, every
seed step blocks on the upstream parse failure.

**Signature takes `projectRoot` and `mcpConfigPath` only.** The
pipeline needs the host project root to derive the brain.aide path
at `.aide/config/brain.aide` and the MCP config path to plan the
brain MCP entry's destination. There is no third positional
parameter, no optional `brainPath`, no caller-supplied path of any
kind. The bundled template helper takes only the inputs it actually
needs to emit its bytes (no `brainPath` argument either); the path
slot inside the helper's emitted args is a static YAML null in
every cold-install case.

## Good examples

A cold-install run on a fresh project. The agent has just executed
`/aide:brain config` for the first time; the router unconditionally
calls `aide_init({ category: "brain" })`, which calls
provisionBrain. provisionBrain returns seven InitStep items in
order:

**Step 1 — Brain config.** Status: would-create. Category: brain.
Name: "Brain config (brain.aide)". FilePath:
`.aide/config/brain.aide` inside the host project root. Content:
the full canonical brain.aide bytes from the bundled template
helper, which on POSIX looks like:

```
---
name: obsidian
mcpServerConfig:
  command: npx
  args:
    - "-y"
    - "@bitbonsai/mcpvault"
    - "--vault"
    -
---

<!-- aide-orientation-start -->
Your brain is an Obsidian-backed knowledge store. Use
`mcp__brain__read_note` to open files by their brain-relative
path. Use `mcp__brain__search_notes` for keyword queries across
every file in the store. Start from the entry-point artifact
matching your task: the coding-playbook index at
`coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`, the study-playbook
navigation guide at `coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`, the
update-playbook maintenance guide at
`coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`, or the research index at
`research/research.md`. Follow the references each entry-point
lists to deepen context. Stay in scope.
<!-- aide-orientation-end -->

<!-- aide-config-start -->
You are completing the wiring of an Obsidian brain. The
unwired-slot signal is YAML null at `mcpServerConfig.args[3]`;
the required value is the absolute path to the user's Obsidian
vault.

Argument shape: `/aide:brain config [<absolute-path>]`. When
`$ARGUMENTS` is non-empty, treat it as the absolute path the user
wants to wire. Empty `$ARGUMENTS` against an already-wired brain
means "STOP, nothing to do".

(Full prose-driven wiring flow continues here in the bundled
template — read brain.aide, decide target path, edit args[3] from
null to the absolute path string, run sync, emit the restart
message.)
<!-- aide-config-end -->

<!-- aide-playbook-index-start -->
# Coding Playbook

## Task Routing

| Task domain | Section |
|-------------|---------|

## Always Read First

(content the user fills in)

## Sections

## Contents
<!-- aide-playbook-index-end -->

<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->
# Study Playbook

## How to use the playbook entry-point

**Step 1.** Open the playbook entry-point artifact. The Task
Routing table tells you which section applies to your task.

**Step 2.** Drill into the section that matches. Read the section
header and the references it lists; pick the references whose
names or descriptions match the task at hand.

**Step 3.** Follow each picked reference into its target. Read
only what your task demands.

## Navigation Rules

The playbook entry-point and section indexes are navigation, not
content — depth does not start there. Depth starts on the first
content reference you reach. From any content reference, follow
the references it lists for at least one or two more levels in
any direction the information could apply, but stop at the first
reference whose target is unrelated to the current task.
<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->

<!-- aide-update-playbook-start -->
# Update Playbook

Maintenance methodology for the coding playbook. Use
`mcp__brain__read_note` to read entries, `mcp__brain__patch_note`
or `mcp__brain__write_note` to edit them. Playbook entries live
under `coding-playbook/<section>/`; the index sits at
`coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`.

1. Identify the change — new convention, modification, section
   rename, section removal, or general audit.
2. Read `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md` to identify the
   relevant section.
3. Apply the change.
4. **Routing-table drift check (required):** compare the playbook
   entry-point's task routing table against the actual sections
   that now exist; reconcile any drift.
5. Confirm the final state.
<!-- aide-update-playbook-end -->

<!-- aide-research-index-start -->
# Research

## Domains

| Domain | Files |
|--------|-------|

## How to Use This Index

Research material is filed by domain. Drill into the domain that
matches your task; do not flat-search across every domain.
<!-- aide-research-index-end -->
```

The frontmatter has exactly two top-level fields: `name` (a label
only; the package never branches on it) and `mcpServerConfig`.
`mcpServerConfig.args` is typed `(string | null)[]`; the bundled
template lands YAML null at `args[3]` (the path slot for the
Obsidian default — the bare `-` line in the YAML emits a null
scalar). The body has exactly six marker-bounded sections in
fixed order, each opened and closed by its own paired
HTML-comment markers. The agent-facing sections (`orientation`,
`config`) are not read by this module — they belong to
`aide_brain`. The four install-time-seed sections (`playbookIndex`,
`studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`) are this
module's read surface and seed the four required entry-point
artifacts in the brain root.

**Step 2 — Brain root directories.** Status: would-create.
Category: brain. FilePath: the brain root path read from the
scaffolded brain.aide. (At cold-install time, that path slot is
YAML null, so this step's filePath reflects the unwired-brain-root
state; the directory creation cannot proceed until sync runs
against a brain.aide whose path slot has been filled in by the
agent.)

**Step 3 — Playbook entry-point artifact.** Status: would-create.
Category: brain. FilePath: `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`
under the brain root. Content: sourced from
`parseBrainAideFromString(brainAideStep.content).playbookIndex` —
the verbatim bytes between `<!-- aide-playbook-index-start -->`
and `<!-- aide-playbook-index-end -->` in the brain.aide that
Step 1 just planned. The bytes are not a copy of an inline
TypeScript constant in this module; they come from the brain.aide
`playbookIndex` section, full stop.

**Step 4 — Study-playbook entry-point artifact.** Status:
would-create. Category: brain. FilePath:
`coding-playbook/study-playbook.md` under the brain root. Content:
sourced from
`parseBrainAideFromString(brainAideStep.content).studyPlaybook`.
Same parser pass as Step 3, different typed key. The artifact at
this path is the on-disk surface the agent-side `study-playbook`
skill points at.

**Step 5 — Update-playbook entry-point artifact.** Status:
would-create. Category: brain. FilePath:
`coding-playbook/update-playbook.md` under the brain root —
sibling of the study-playbook artifact. Content: sourced from
`parseBrainAideFromString(brainAideStep.content).updatePlaybook`.
The artifact at this path is the on-disk surface the shipped
`/aide:update-playbook` slash command points at; the command file
stays minimal because the maintenance methodology lives here.

**Step 6 — Research entry-point artifact.** Status: would-create.
Category: brain. FilePath: `research/research.md` under the brain
root. Content: sourced from
`parseBrainAideFromString(brainAideStep.content).researchIndex`.

**Step 7 — MCP config (brain).** Status: would-create. Category:
mcp. Prescription derived from the same Step 1 content bytes via
parseBrainAide + interpolateArgs. The args carry the YAML null at
index 3 verbatim through the parser and the interpolator into the
prescription:

```
{
  key: "brain",
  entry: {
    command: "npx",
    args: ["-y", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "--vault", null]
  }
}
```

The null entry rides through unchanged. The InitStep is staged-
only: sync (downstream of `/aide:brain config` after the agent
fills the null) refuses to write null-bearing args to `.mcp.json`,
so a partially-wired brain configuration cannot silently land. The
agent's next move — driven verbatim by the integration's `config`
body section that `aide_brain({ kind: "config" })` returns — is to
edit `args[3]` in brain.aide from null to the user's absolute
path, then re-run sync. After that edit, parseBrainAide returns a
`(string | string)[]` for args, interpolateArgs hands sync a
null-free prescription, and `.mcp.json["brain"]` lands.

**Post-execution state of the brain root after a fresh install
completes (all seven steps applied, agent has filled the null,
sync has run).** Four entry-point artifact files are seeded into
the brain root at their canonical Obsidian-default paths
(`coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`,
`coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`,
`coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`, `research/research.md`).
Each is readable via `mcp__brain__read_note`. brain.aide is on
disk under `.aide/config/brain.aide` with the user's absolute
path now at `args[3]`; the `.mcp.json["brain"]` entry has the
same path; the brain MCP server launches cleanly on the next IDE
restart. Re-running `/aide:brain config` at this point: all
seven steps return `exists`. The brain.aide presence check
fires (file is on disk under `.aide/config/`); the brain root
directories are present; the four entry-point artifacts are
present (presence-only check on each, no byte comparison
against the current brain.aide section that originated them);
the MCP config `brain` key matches the derived entry.

**Self-heal scenario: user accidentally deletes
`coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`.** The user re-runs
`/aide:brain config` (with no arguments — the brain is already
wired, so the integration's config prose enters the
"already-wired" branch). The router unconditionally calls
`aide_init({ category: "brain" })` regardless of brain status.
provisionBrain re-runs its seven-step plan: brain.aide is present
(`exists`); brain root directories are present (`exists`); the
playbook entry-point artifact's file is missing on disk
(`would-create`, with content sourced from
`parseBrainAideFromString(currentBrainAide).playbookIndex`); the
study-playbook, update-playbook, and research artifacts are
present (`exists`); the MCP entry matches (`exists`). The apply
layer writes only the missing playbook artifact; the user's other
seed artifacts — including any customizations they made to those
artifacts post-install — are not touched. Presence-only seed-
semantic idempotency makes the unconditional `aide_init` call safe
to run on every `/aide:brain config` invocation; the missing-file
re-seed is exactly the recovery path the rule was designed for.

## Bad examples

**provisionBrain accepting a `brainPath` parameter.** Any signature
that takes a caller-supplied brain root — on the orchestrator, on
a per-step builder, or on the bundled template helper — is the
pre-pivot shape brought back through the side door:

```
// Wrong — caller-supplied brain root reintroduces the retired path-routing
export async function provisionBrain(
  projectRoot: string,
  brainPath: string | undefined, // ← parameter does not exist
  mcpConfigPath: string,
): Promise<InitStep[]> { ... }

function buildPlaybookStep(brainPath: string, parsed: ParsedBrainAide) { ... }

function bundledBrainAideTemplate(brainPath: string | undefined): string { ... }
```

cli/init no longer calls provisionBrain. The only call site is
`aide_init({ category: "brain" })` from `/aide:brain config`, and
that call site has no brain path to thread — the bundled template
emits YAML null at the path slot regardless of caller, and the
agent fills the null in by direct edit to brain.aide post-scaffold.
Any `if (brainPath) { ... } else { ... }` branch, any helper-helper
pass-through, and any
`(projectRoot, brainPath?, mcpConfigPath)`-shaped signature
restores the retired in-code path-routing the schema collapse
already retired. The signature is `(projectRoot, mcpConfigPath)`
only; the bundled template helper takes only its content inputs.

**The bundled template emitting a literal-string sentinel instead
of YAML null.**

```
// Wrong — literal-string placeholder at the path slot
return `---
name: obsidian
mcpServerConfig:
  command: npx
  args:
    - "-y"
    - "@bitbonsai/mcpvault"
    - "--vault"
    - "<BRAIN_PATH>"   // ← string, not YAML null
---
...`;
```

The unwired-slot signal is YAML null, full stop:
`mcpServerConfig.args` is typed `(string | null)[]` and the
typing is the contract. A literal-string sentinel re-couples the
tooling to a "knows what an unwired path looks like" code path and
re-creates the byte-stable failure mode the rework was built to
close: a `.mcp.json` brain entry that already carried the
sentinel verbatim would byte-match an MCP step computed against a
re-scaffolded sentinel-bearing brain.aide, the drift check would
pass, sync would write the sentinel to disk, and the brain MCP
server would fail to launch until the user noticed. The same
rejection applies to any other shape — `<PATH>`, `__BRAIN__`, an
empty string, dropping the entry from the array entirely, or a
`${...}` placeholder. None of those are the structural unwired-
slot signal; YAML null is.

**provisionBrain reading the live agent-facing sections.**

```
// Wrong — destructures keys that belong to aide_brain
const parsed = parseBrainAideFromString(brainAideStep.content);
const { orientation, config, playbookIndex } = parsed;

steps.push({
  name: "Brain root context note",
  content: orientation, // ← seeding orientation as a brain-root file
});
```

The `orientation` and `config` typed keys are read by `aide_brain`
at runtime and by no one else. provisionBrain reads ONLY the
install-time-seed keys (`playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
`updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`). Naming `orientation` or
`config` as a parseBrainAide result field, destructuring either,
or passing either to a downstream helper crosses the
per-consumer-ownership line — even transitively, even to validate,
even to log. The four seed sections reach the agent only through
the on-disk artifacts this module plans; the two live sections
reach the agent only through `aide_brain`'s `kind` parameter.

**A step builder hardcoding seed-artifact bytes as inline
TypeScript constants.**

```
// Wrong — entry-point artifact bytes outside the brain.aide body
const PLAYBOOK_TEMPLATE = `# Coding Playbook\n\n## Task Routing...`;
const STUDY_PLAYBOOK_TEMPLATE = `# Study Playbook\n\n## Step 1...`;
const UPDATE_PLAYBOOK_TEMPLATE = `# Update Playbook\n\n## Maintenance...`;
const RESEARCH_TEMPLATE = `# Research\n\n## Domains...`;

steps.push({ name: "Playbook entry-point", content: PLAYBOOK_TEMPLATE });
```

A user who edits their brain.aide's `playbookIndex`,
`studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, or `researchIndex` body section
finds the next run ignores their edit and lands the package
default again. Entry-point artifact bytes belong in the named
marker-bounded brain.aide body sections and nowhere else in this
module; reading them back through parseBrainAide is the only path
this module sees those bytes. Duplicating any of them as inline
constants — under any name, under any "temporary" rationale —
creates two sources of truth for the same bytes.

**A step builder returning `would-overwrite` for an entry-point
artifact whose disk bytes drift from the brain.aide section.**

```
// Wrong — byte-compares a user-customized seed artifact
const onDisk = await readFile(playbookArtifactPath, "utf8");
const fromTemplate = parsed.playbookIndex;
if (onDisk !== fromTemplate) {
  return {
    name: "Playbook entry-point artifact",
    filePath: playbookArtifactPath,
    status: "would-overwrite",
    content: fromTemplate,
  };
}
```

The user has been editing their playbook for weeks — adding their
own routing rows, their own conventions, their own structure. On
the next `/aide:brain config`, they are asked "overwrite your
playbook?" A reflexive Enter replaces their work with the bundled
default. Presence-only seed-semantic idempotency exists exactly
to make the unconditional `aide_init` call self-heal-only:
present means `exists`, absent means `would-create`, no byte
comparison. Byte-comparison idempotency would degrade the
unconditional self-heal into a continual nag, and the rule
applies uniformly across all four entry-point artifacts.

**A step builder returning `would-overwrite` for any path under
`.aide/config/`.**

```
// Wrong — byte-compares a user-owned config file
const disk = await readFile(brainAidePath, "utf8");
const template = bundledBrainAideTemplate();
if (disk !== template) {
  return {
    name: "Brain config (brain.aide)",
    filePath: brainAidePath,
    status: "would-overwrite",
    content: template,
  };
}
```

The user edited their brain.aide to fill the null at args[3]
with their absolute path. On the next init, the byte-comparison
fails and `would-overwrite` reverts the wired path back to YAML
null. The `.aide/config/` directory is a load-bearing user-owned
surface inherited from the root spec; per-file allowlists are
forbidden, and no code path in this module is allowed to return
`would-overwrite` for any path inside it — even to "fix" a stale
template, land newer defaults, or tidy formatting.

**provisionBrain "defensively" replacing a null at plan time.**

```
// Wrong — silently substitutes a fallback for the null slot
const parsed = parseBrainAideFromString(brainAideStep.content);
const args = parsed.frontmatter.mcpServerConfig.args.map((a) =>
  a === null ? join(homedir(), "brain") : a,
);
steps.push({
  name: "MCP config (brain)",
  prescription: { key: "brain", entry: { command: parsed.frontmatter.mcpServerConfig.command, args } },
});
```

The null is the structural unwired-slot signal — provisionBrain
forwards it verbatim through parseBrainAide and interpolateArgs
into the MCP-step InitStep, and sync (downstream) refuses to
write null-bearing args to `.mcp.json`. Defensive null-replacement
re-couples the install pipeline to a "knows what an unwired slot
looks like" code path and masks user wiring mistakes that the
sync-refusal surfaces loudly. The path-correctness handoff is the
agent's responsibility, driven by the integration's `config` body
section that `aide_brain({ kind: "config" })` returns; not this
module's.

**cli/init calling provisionBrain directly.**

```
// Wrong — cli/init reaches into the brain category
import { provisionBrain } from "../service/install/provisionBrain";

export async function init() {
  const projectRoot = process.cwd();
  const mcpConfigPath = resolveMcpConfigPath(projectRoot);
  const brainSteps = await provisionBrain(projectRoot, mcpConfigPath); // ← cli/init does not call this
  steps.push(...brainSteps);
}
```

cli/init is argument-less and brain-agnostic by design. It ships
the methodology layer only — docs, agents, commands, skills,
framework-pointer stub — and never writes brain.aide, never writes
a `.mcp.json` brain entry, and never asks for a brain path. Brain
wiring is fully delegated to `/aide:brain config` on first `/aide`
run, and the brain category of `aide_init` is reached only from
that router. cli/init reaching into provisionBrain re-couples the
cold-install path to brain wiring — the exact coupling the
delegation was built to remove.

**A provisionBrain that names retired typed-key vocabulary in any
read path.**

```
// Wrong — retired typed keys re-entering the module's read path
const parsed = parseBrainAideFromString(brainAideStep.content);
const { prose, playbook, research } = parsed; // ← all retired names

steps.push({ name: "Playbook entry-point", content: parsed["playbook"] });
```

The retired typed-key vocabulary (`prose`, `playbook` as a typed
key, `research` as a typed key) is gone from this module's read
paths. The rename to `orientation` (which `aide_brain` owns now,
not this module) and `playbookIndex` / `researchIndex` (this
module's typed keys) is load-bearing because it disambiguates
each section's role: orientation is a runtime briefing; the
`*Index` suffix marks navigable surfaces whose content the user
fills. Reintroducing the retired names — even via a string-keyed
access like `result["playbook"]` — restores the pre-rework
vocabulary and is rejected.

**A provisionBrain that scaffolds a brain-root CLAUDE.md.**

```
// Wrong — CLAUDE.md is no longer a framework requirement
steps.push({
  name: "Brain-root CLAUDE.md",
  filePath: join(brainRoot, "CLAUDE.md"),
  content: BRAIN_CLAUDE_MD_TEMPLATE,
});
```

The framework's brain contract is exactly four required entry-
point artifacts (playbook + study-playbook + update-playbook +
research). Reintroducing a CLAUDE.md scaffolding step under any
name restores the retired contract.

**A provisionBrain using storage-specific identifiers in code.**

```
// Wrong — connector-specific tokens in code identifiers
async function vaultExists(brainRoot: string): Promise<boolean> { ... }
const VAULT_DIRS = ["research", "process/retro", "coding-playbook"];
const obsidianHubPath = join(brainRoot, "coding-playbook", "coding-playbook.md");
```

The structural concepts the module handles are "brain root",
"playbook entry-point artifact", "study-playbook entry-point
artifact", "update-playbook entry-point artifact", and "research
entry-point artifact" — how those manifest in the user's chosen
storage is brain.aide's concern, not this module's code surface.

**A brain.aide scaffolded with extra frontmatter fields beyond
the collapsed schema.**

```
---
name: obsidian
connector: obsidian            # Wrong — field renamed away; `name` is the only label
rootPath: D:/notes/my-brain    # Wrong — path lives inline in args, not as its own field
entryFile: CLAUDE.md           # Wrong — no code consumes this
mcpServerConfig:
  command: npx
  args: ["-y", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "--vault", null]
---
```

Each removed field is validation theater — a slot the user is
nagged to fill in with no code path that consumes the value once
filled. Every frontmatter field must justify itself by naming a
real runtime consumer (sync writes `mcpServerConfig`; the user
and agent read `name` as a label).

## References

- `.aide/intent.aide` (parent spec) — Established the load-bearing
  contract this module enforces: brain.aide as the single source
  of truth for brain configuration, the collapsed two-field
  schema (`name` + `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}`),
  `mcpServerConfig.args` typed `(string | null)[]` with YAML null
  as the explicit unwired-slot signal, the `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`
  default launcher, the `.aide/config/` directory ownership
  boundary, the sync contract as the only mutation surface for
  `.mcp.json` (refusing to write null-bearing args), the cold-
  install CLI being argument-less and brain-agnostic, and the
  unconditional `aide_init({ category: "brain" })` call from
  `/aide:brain config` as the missing-file self-heal mechanism.
  Drove the no-`brainPath`-parameter signature, the YAML-null-
  not-sentinel decision, the directory-wide ownership invariant,
  the schema-minimum-equals-maximum rule, the
  no-connector-branching constraint that keeps identifier naming
  storage-agnostic, and the plans/agent-fills/sync-writes
  hand-off shape that frames every Strategy paragraph.
- `.aide/intent.aide` (parent outcome on the body schema) —
  Established that `brain.aide`'s body is parsed as six named
  marker-bounded sections delimited by paired HTML-comment
  markers in fixed order, partitioned by reader: `orientation`
  and `config` for `aide_brain`; `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
  `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex` for this module. Marker
  layout violations halt the pipeline as `malformed-body`. Drove
  the per-consumer-ownership Strategy beat, the six-section
  worked Good example, the strict-failure migration paragraph,
  and the Bad-example for `provisionBrain` reading the live
  agent-facing keys.
- `.aide/intent.aide` (parent outcome on storage-agnostic
  vocabulary) — Established the forbidden-token list (`vault`,
  `obsidian`, `note`-as-synonym, `wikilink`, `hub note`, `hub`),
  the rule that those tokens must not appear in code or
  user-facing prose, and the user-owned-section-bytes carve-out
  permitting the bundled obsidian-flavored default to ship
  connector-specific prose inside the user-owned seed sections.
  Drove the Strategy beat on storage-agnostic vocabulary and the
  Bad-example for connector-specific identifier naming.
- `.aide/intent.aide` (parent outcome on the unconditional
  `aide_init` call) — Established that `/aide:brain config` calls
  `aide_init({ category: "brain" })` UNCONDITIONALLY at the top
  of every invocation — not gated, not branched, not skipped —
  and that presence-only seed-semantic idempotency is what makes
  the unconditional call safe by self-healing missing files
  without touching customized ones. Drove the Strategy paragraph
  on presence-only idempotency, the self-heal Good example
  (deleted playbook artifact), and the Bad-example for
  byte-comparison idempotency on entry-point artifacts.
- `src/service/parseBrainAide/.aide` — Sibling spec: the parser
  that reads brain.aide and returns typed frontmatter
  (`mcpServerConfig.args` typed `(string | null)[]`) plus the
  six named body fields, plus the `interpolateArgs` helper that
  walks the args carrying null entries through unchanged. The
  parser's per-consumer-ownership outcomes name provisionBrain
  as the consumer that reads `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
  `updatePlaybook`, AND `researchIndex` to source the four
  required entry-point artifact bodies; the symmetric inverse
  names `aide_brain` as the sole consumer of `orientation` and
  `config`. Drove the brain.aide-as-source-of-truth Strategy
  beat and the typed-key-level enforcement of per-consumer
  ownership.
- `src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/`
  — Sibling helper: the bundled default brain.aide template.
  Owns the collapsed two-field frontmatter, the platform-
  branched launcher invocation, the static YAML null at the path
  slot of `mcpServerConfig.args`, and the byte content of all
  six marker-bounded body sections — including the `config`
  section (the Obsidian wiring flow `aide_brain` reads live to
  drive null-fill) and the `update-playbook` section (the
  playbook-maintenance methodology this module seeds). The
  helper takes no `brainPath` argument; the path slot is
  structurally null in every emission. Drove the Bad-example
  for emitting a literal-string sentinel and the Strategy beat
  that no `brainPath` parameter exists at any layer.
- `src/service/install/.aide` (sibling-cascade ancestor) —
  Established that the install service is consumer-agnostic and
  shared by `aide_init` and the npx CLI, that the brain MCP
  entry's command/args are derived from the user-owned
  brain.aide via parseBrainAide rather than constructed inline,
  and that the package never owns launcher bytes. Drove the
  Strategy beat that brain.aide is the single source of truth
  for launcher bytes and the Bad-example for inline launcher
  reconstruction.
- `.aide/handoff.aide` — Architectural snapshot: history of the
  brain-layer schema cycle, including the identifier-naming
  sweep landed in code (`vaultExists` → `brainRootExists`,
  `VAULT_DIRS` → `BRAIN_ROOT_DIRS`), the move from inline
  launcher constants to template-helper-owned launcher bytes,
  the deletion of the `brainPath` parameter chain, and the
  pivot from a literal-string placeholder to YAML null.
  Confirms the Bad-example identifier scenario stays as a
  regression-against-rollback rather than a description of
  current code; informed the YAML-null-not-sentinel rationale
  paragraph in Strategy.
- `.aide/docs/brain-aide.md` — Methodology doc: brain.aide file
  format, frontmatter field semantics, the marker-bounded body
  section vocabulary, the typed `(string | null)[]` shape of
  `mcpServerConfig.args` with YAML null as the unwired-slot
  signal, and the divergence from intent-spec conventions that
  makes scope/intent/outcomes fields explicitly absent. Drove
  the Bad-example for borrowing intent-spec frontmatter fields
  and for adding validation-theater fields beyond the collapsed
  schema.
